  "design": ["§5 step 3: build query syntax from escaped literal terms (an AST), never from caller-supplied MATCH expressions — the F4 class of defect", "§5 step 4 (03 rev 7.8, 09 §6.5.2): phrases and exact identifiers matched as phrases on the `unicode61` table, the last token a prefix only when it has three or more characters and is not a stop word, and as literals on the header trigram table; a stop word routed alone is never a prefix"],
  "loads_in": ["slice-2"],
  "notes_for_implementer": "The target note contains `and` twice and `or` once; the garden note is its decoy, and the linker and phone notes are the decoys of steps 19 and 20 (added by LQ17, 09 §6.5.6). Every query below must run without an FTS5 syntax error and return the target whenever at least one of its literal terms of three or more characters occurs in it as a word or a word's beginning — union semantics. `and`, `not` and `near` are three-letter literals that occur (or do not) in the text like any other word: `and` matches, `NOT` does not occur and is simply a non-matching term. `tabs*` is the word `tabs` with its punctuation trimmed. Since LQ17 the index sends every word of three or more characters to the unicode61 table as a prefix of its own, so step 9 can no longer tell a caller's `*` taken as syntax from the index's prefix; step 19 can: `x_ab*` is the literal `x_ab`, whose last token `ab` has two characters and so is never a prefix, and the note holding `x_abc` must not be found — only an injected `*` would find it (the current layout found it as a substring). Step 20: `and` routed alone is a stop word, matched exactly, so it finds the note that says `and` and not the one that says `Android`. `title:tabs` is not a column filter: the term `title:tabs` is a literal phrase. Two-character queries (`or`) go to the substring pass. The garden decoy never appears.",
